  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nDry conditions with above normal temperatures persist through \nthe weekend. This will exacerbate ongoing water resource drought\nconditions.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nDeep layered ridging builds in today through Thursday. A\nnorthern stream shortwave working its way over the top of the\nridge tonight could bring some light rain showers and/or \nsprinkles across the Saint Johns Valley. Otherwise it will be\ndry through Thursday due to subsidence under the ridge.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nThere may be a chance for some precipitation early next week \nbefore high pressure returns. Any drought relief is unlikely.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nA trough will dig into the northeast Monday, at the same time a\ncoastal low moves up the east coast. How these two features \ninteract will be the primary factor that determines QPF values \nacross the region. Most recent deterministic guidance has slowed\ndown the timing of the progression of both the trough and the \ncoastal low, allowing the low to come closer to the Maine coast.\nMore GEFS members are starting to catch on to this trend as \nwell. This scenario would increase QPF, especially for coastal \nregions. There is still significant uncertainty as this time and\nmodel trends will need to be closely monitored over the coming \ndays. \n\nFollowing the passage of the front, there is strong agreement \namong ensembles that Canadian high pressure will move in, \nbringing cold and dry weather through Black Friday.",
